k contains only wax and there's  no tissue left  so in cases like this when where there  is not enough tissue for testing  um it is recommended  that either a repeat biopsy or liquid  testing  be performed this is recommended by both  the  cap islac amp  and nccn guidelines  and on a related note  testing rates for recommended biomarkers  is um not that great even after  you know all this time that we've been  performing them  there was a study presented at asco  last year showing that a group of  patients  1200 patients at five different  hospitals  only about half of them had been tested  for egfr  alk ross1 and pdl1 and the rate is  much lower for the other biomarkers  so the nccn cap is like an amp  clinical practice guidelines call for  the preservation of tissue for molecular  testing  and for pathologists to make a diagnosis  based  on morphology whenever possible  and for the judicious use of  immunohistochemistry  using only p40 and tcf1  immunohistochemicals genes to classify  most nscc cases  and if we follow these recommendations  for  fostering tissue stewardship  it is possible to turn your mole hills  into mountains  and a mole hill of a biopsy into a  mountain of testing results  in our lab we have implemented  the specimen handling protocol shown  here  there is no tissue waste  we start by saving the effacement  shavings in a vial and it's  this little picture of the vial and the  the  shavings are represented by the  um blue lines  on the left um the next uh  couple of sections are used for h e  and two is a  perfect amount is is exactly what is  needed to make the morphologic  assessment  no more than that is necessary the next  set of sections  are for immunohistochemical stains and  pdl1  analysis the following set  is for  molecular testing um and then  followed by fish and we also have  a quality control h e slide  cut just before the molecular  unstained sections so  by following this protocol along with  the clinical practice guidelines for