gic malignancies  briefly myeloid neoplasms represent  a highly heterogeneous group of diseases  that encompass many different groups of  diseases the diagnostic workup  of patients with these disorders is very  labor intense  and operationally complex and in this  presentation  i will be discussing the  perspective on the clinical impact of  the recent advancements  in ngs testing  this slide demonstrates the complexity  of the current who 2017  classification of myeloid malignancies  um as you can see and many of you are  familiar with  i want you to appreciate that many of  the  specific entities um in  under the rubric of myeloid malignancies  are characterized by a diverse  um group of uh genetic alterations  involving gene rearrangements  mutations copy number variation  and we recognize today that the  contribution of the genetic  abnormalities  play a critical role in the  classification of these myeloid  malignancies  in this slide i will demonstrate that  myeloid neuroplasms are heterogeneous  at many levels clinically  pathologically and genetically  many of these are associated with  characteristic and sometimes  overlapping genetic alterations which  are increasingly playing a critical role  in the diagnosis of classification  treatment and risk stratification of  this diseases  the large groups of myeloid neoplasms  are composed of the chronic myelo  proliferation  neoplasms like cml  polycythemia and others that are defined  by either the dish or able rearrangement  and mutations involving three genes  jack ii calm reticulin and nipple  myelodysplastic syndromes also are  characterized by  mutations in certain genes mds  npn overlapping syndromes also have  uh characteristic gene alterations some  of them  are shared between the myeloproductive  neoplasms  and the myelodysplastic syndromes and  then the group of  acute myeloid leukemias many of which  are characterized by both fusion genes  um as well as mutations  over the last 20 years through  cyto