e and iterative process  to establish the diagnosis and identify  risk predictors and identify therapeutic  options  and disease monitoring is quite a  labor-intensive process  that requires a lot of samples material  because of the way it is  carried out currently and so in addition  to the  operational and logistic complexity  there's also the added complexity that  each of these  testing modalities have variable  turnaround times and diagnostic yield  and many all of these contribute to  results where sometimes we will run out  of samples  as well as repeat biopsies from patients  of these precious  material so this is  a slide to demonstrate the multiple  levels of complexity  there we need to discuss the specimen  quality space requirement  time of ordering time of testing time of  result availability  information systems and personnel  coordination  in all of these laboratories to  coordinate the  appropriate and timely testing of all of  these  analytes all of these requires multiple  levels of coordination and result  integration that requires time  and before we can deliver accurate and  timely  information that will impact the  delivery of  precision oncology this is a figure  to demonstrate some of the logistic  complexity  and the complicated workflow many of  these specimens do  come to the clinical lab via  many different routes there is a process  called triaging where we have to assess  the morphologic  um evaluation of the specimens  some of them are then processed in the  surgical pathology  department or division and then  followed by assessment in the  hematopathology  laboratory this highlights that  the logistic complexity may result in  specimen dispersal on dilution through  many different buildings and multiple  labs  results in multiple accession numbers  given to one patient  biopsy material results in  up to eight modalities of testing with  variable specimen needs  with prolonged turnaround times and many  many different hands  that are 